+# Runbook: OTA dual-partition updates, staged canaries & rollback
+
+Purpose:
+- Safely roll out model updates to medical devices (imaging workstations, edge appliances) using dual-partition updates and a staged canary strategy.
+
+Deployment strategy:
+1. Build and sign artifact, produce evidence bundle and manifest (TUF-like).
+2. Canary stage: publish manifest with `canary: true` and target a small subset (1-5%) of devices/testbeds.
+3. Monitor device health, model smoke tests and clinical metrics for canary period (e.g., 24-72h).
+4. If canary is healthy, progressively increase percentage to 25%, 50%, then 100%.
+
+Dual-partition update pattern:
+- Device has `active` and `staging` partitions. Update writes to `staging`, runs smoke tests, and only switches if tests pass (see edge/ota/dual_partition_update.sh).
+
+Rollback procedure:
+ - If smoke tests or metrics indicate failure, mark manifest superseded and publish rollback manifest pointing to previous artifact.
+ - Devices detect superseded manifest and roll back to previous active partition.
+ - Escalation: if >X% devices fail, run emergency rollback across fleet and notify Clinical Ops.
+
+Validation drills:
+- Before fleet rollout, test rollback on representative devices and measure rollback time (target < 15 minutes).
+- Document results and sign off with Clinical Ops.
+
+Monitoring:
+- Monitor model_load_success, verification_failures, clinical outcome deltas and device health metrics (CPU, memory, disk).
+
